oung black figure skaters is mabel  fairbanks  [Music]  forbidden the practice or compete  because of her race  mabel fairbanks taught herself on frozen  ponds and flooded tennis courts  she gave you courage strength  validity fairbanks coached a toy wilson  he was the first black figure skater to  compete he went on to win  a national title in 1966.  is there something poetic about the fact  that mabel fairbanks  fell in love with figure skating in  harlem  and there's now this organization in  harlem  teaching young and brown girls  to see this link really paying homage  to the trailblazing people such as mabel  fairbanks on ice she was alone by  herself  out there but had the distinction and  tenacity  and the enthusiasm  as it is in a relationship of seeing  the girls with figure skating in harlem  going to competitions and seeing that  we were the only majority black team  our coaches always reinforcing in us you  worked hard to get here  i work hard to be in these spaces i  deserve to just  to feel just as beautiful and just as  elegant  over the past five years 100 of the  students in the program  went on to college alumni have become  successful lawyers  teachers photographers and entrepreneurs  what are some of the lessons  that you learned here that you'll pick  up  long after you've left this program and  you turn up in a board room and you're  the only black executive there  my family has made me strong my  community has made me strong i have made  myself strong and i choose to be strong  in the spaces that i choose to be in any  room that i walk into i know i have  something special  to give  [Music]  back in 2018 beyonce's ivy park  sportswear line honored figure skating  in harlem because  quote these girls embody our motto  strong beyond measure 250 girls in that  program wow  they have a smaller program in detroit  they've helped a lot of people uh learn  to skate and  improve themselves and did you know  about mabel fairbanks no i didn't  ever hear